From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:44:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: keep the HDMI FRL caps of a sink that isn't ready for
 link training

When a display wakes up, the probe clears the FRL caps and assesses the link
again. If the sink isn't ready yet or no SOR can be assigned, the caps stay
empty, the FRL-only modes leave the mode list, and the compositor can't set its
mode again until the display is probed again. Reported in upstream PR 1505 on an
Odyssey G95NC at 7680x2160, where the sink takes a couple of seconds to get
ready. Failed link training of a modeset also reassesses the link right away
since "report failed HDMI FRL link training of a modeset", which can empty the
caps the same way.

Save the caps of each successful link training with the EDID they apply to.
When an assessment fails and the EDID is the same, go back to them. The HDMI
library reports success when training failed at every rate, so count that as a
failure too.

The caps stay in use for the first two failed modesets in a row, as each retry
gives a slow sink more time. The third one drops them, so that the compositor
falls back to TMDS on a link that really is dead. A display that is off is
assessed again on the next query. For a display in use, the link training of its
next modeset decides.

Also let a retrain request recover the rate of a link that failed training while
the display is on TMDS. "Don't replace an assessed HDMI FRL rate with the EDID
rate" made that rate stay at none until the display was probed again.

Unlike the PR, this has no module parameters and doesn't change the 200 ms
between link training attempts, which is shorter than that sink needs: it would
take a retry from the compositor there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
